# .claude/ -- ignored by CONTENTS, not as a directory.
#
# `.claude/` on its own excludes the directory, and git then never descends into
# it, so a later `!.claude/hooks/` re-include is silently ignored. Excluding
# `.claude/*` instead leaves the directory walkable and makes the exceptions
# below actually take effect. Verified with `git check-ignore -v`.
.claude/*

# EXCEPT the design-lookup gate. An untracked gate protects only the machine it
# was written on: a runner box, a Codex worker, a fresh clone, or a worktree
# gets no enforcement at all -- which is exactly where an unresearched edit is
# most expensive and least visible. The gate and the settings that wire it up
# are repo policy, not machine config, so they ship with the repo.
# settings.local.json stays ignored: permissions and paths ARE machine-local.
!.claude/hooks/
!.claude/settings.json

# Session report buffers are local-only, never committed (public repo)
_reports/

# Added by ggshield
.cache_ggshield

# qmd project-local search index: the database is rebuildable
# and never committed; the allowlist config is tracked.
.qmd/*
!.qmd/index.yml

# Capture watermark store: the Python capture hooks default their SQLite
# read-offset store to PACKAGE_ROOT/data/capture-watermarks.sqlite
# (openbrain.config CaptureSettings.watermark_path), so running a hook from the
# source tree creates this dir. It is per-machine runtime state, rebuildable by
# re-reading transcripts, and never belongs in git.
python/openbrain/data/

# Secrets and file-based config layers: ALLOWLIST, never a blocklist.
#
# The structure is tracked, the values are not. A blocklist of known-bad names
# (`*.secret.json`, `credentials.json`) only ever catches the spellings someone
# thought of; the first file named something new lands in git. Ignoring the
# whole directory and re-admitting named exceptions inverts that: a new file is
# ignored by default and has to be deliberately allowed.
#
# The committed *.example.json files carry the full key structure with
# placeholder values, so a fresh clone shows exactly what is required without a
# real value ever entering git.
#
# An ignored file is still a file on disk. Check what is already sitting in
# secrets/ before assuming this has covered you.
secrets/*
!secrets/.gitkeep
!secrets/README.md
!secrets/*.example
!secrets/*.example.json

# Deploy stamp written into a DEPLOYED tree by scripts/local-clone-deploy.sh
# and read back by `/health` as `revision`. It describes one deployment, not
# the source, so it must never be committed — a checked-in stamp would make
# every tree claim to be whichever one happened to be deployed when it landed.
.deployed-revision
